In this society, duty is fixed by station and authority, not by personal preference or payment. The town’s leaders owe direct care to the residents currently within their charge, since those people hold specific roles and depend on the order being maintained now. At the same time, authority carries the burden of stewardship: preserving the town’s physical and social conditions so that future generations can assume their proper places in the hierarchy. Personal benefit is irrelevant; what matters is fulfilling one’s role in sustaining the community across time. Thus, completely ignoring present needs violates immediate obligations, but failing to protect the town’s future also betrays the leader’s duty to maintain lasting order. The right path recognizes both claims, with present residents receiving real consideration and long-term preservation being treated as a serious duty of governance.

Judgment: current residents should not be dismissed, yet the authority’s stewardship gives strong weight to protecting the town for those who will later inhabit it, so a balanced decision that still honors those here now is appropriate. Reasoning: direct obligations to the present community matter greatly, but the highest calling of leadership is good stewardship that thinks beyond one generation; extending direct moral claims to people who do not yet hold any station is weaker.

